     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 5,100 mg of metformin a day is about double the maximum recommended dose.\n\n- **Dose:** 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. The usual maximum is 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day, depending on the formulation. A dose this high greatly raises the risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **Ordering authority:** You said you aren't a clinician. Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one.\n\n**Next steps:**\n1. If the intended dose was different, send me the corrected dose. Common regimens are 500 mg twice daily, 850 mg twice daily, or 1000 mg twice daily with meals. The prescriber should confirm it.\n2. If 1700 mg was a typo, for example for 850 mg or 1000 mg, the prescriber should confirm the intended dose.\n3. If the prescriber really intends this dose, they should enter it themselves or confirm it with pharmacy. I can enter it once a prescriber has explicitly confirmed it."
      }
     ],
